UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 1, Lesson 2: Mapping our local area', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nDraw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things must be added so another student can use it?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDrawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a map of the area within five minutes of school or a place you know well. Include eight features, a key, a north arrow and a title. Write two directions such as \"go north along ...\". Leave out your home address.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a map of the area within five minutes of school or a place you know well. Include eight features, a key, a north arrow and a title. Write two directions such as \"go north along ...\". Leave out your home address. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Street runs north to south and the north arrow points to the top. The key says square = home, green = park, S = shop.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Correction: A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Draw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Inquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named sources or places.","question":"Inquire into or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the sources or places that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Street runs north to south and the north arrow points to the top. The key says square = home, green = park, S = shop.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely misconception for this HSIE topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Correction: A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise map.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use map with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify symbol and explain why it matters.","I can practise key independently on a new prompt.","I can notice direction needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Street runs north to south and the north arrow points to the top. The key says square = home, green = park, S = shop.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDraw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things must be added so another student can use it?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.","explain":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A map is a plan from above, not a side picture.","caption":"Plan view shows where things are.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Map</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">view from above</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">side view of houses</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Stree Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure